## Impact

## Customer Impact:
If an attacker gains control of the admin/oracle key, they could:
Manipulate Token Valuation: Set the exchange rate to an artificially high value, making cDCETH appear vastly more valuable than its underlying DAI reserves
Exploit DeFi Integrations: If cDCETH is used as collateral in lending protocols (Aave, Compound, etc.), the attacker could borrow excessive amounts of other assets against inflated collateral value
Cause Unfair Redemptions: Users redeeming cDCETH during the manipulation period would receive significantly fewer underlying tokens than expected
Erode Trust: Discovery of such a basic validation flaw undermines confidence in Crypto.com's security engineering practices

## Business Impact:
Financial Loss: Potential liability from exploited DeFi positions or user losses during manipulation periods
Reputational Damage: Public disclosure of a trivial logic flaw in a core financial contract could deter institutional and retail users
Regulatory Scrutiny: As a regulated entity, Crypto.com may face questions from regulators about inadequate smart contract security controls
Program Credibility: This finding demonstrates that even audited, production contracts can contain basic vulnerabilities, highlighting the value of continuous bug bounty programs


